 [case id=program-eq-element-020-structural level=core
  [tags equality element structural attrs document-order decimal distinct]
  [meta [#
note: element VALUE equality is STRUCTURAL — names, attributes
note: as a name-keyed set (order-insensitive) under scalar rules,
note: children pairwise in DOCUMENT order, recursing. Decimal keeps
note: mathematical value equality inside elements (1.10 = 1.1). The
note: pre-fix arm compared name + child count only, so [x 1] = [x 3]
note: was true across every consumer.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[probe
  a=[= [x 1] [x 3]]
  b=[= [x 1] [x 1]]
  c=[$eq [x a=1] [x a=2]]
  d=[$eq [x a=1] [x a=1]]
  e=[= [p 1.10] [p 1.1]]
  f=[$count [$distinct ([x 1], [x 3], [x 1])]]
  g=[= [x k=1 v=2] [x v=2 k=1]]
  h=[= [x [y 1] [z 2]] [x [z 2] [y 1]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[probe a=false b=true c=false d=true e=true f=2 g=true h=false]
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-eq-021-two-equality-notions level=core
  [tags equality atomization value-eq identity CO-11 990]
  [meta [#
note: RULED: CO-11 — CX has TWO deliberate equality notions. [$eq] is
note: VALUE equality under the atomization policy (compare atomizes):
note: a decimal attribute and the string of its image compare EQUAL,
note: an atom and its same-named string compare EQUAL. The type-
note: faithful IDENTITY notion (strict-canonical bytes: cx eq, cx
note: hash, structural node equality) DISTINGUISHES both pairs and
note: mints distinct addresses — pinned per kind by the CO-12 image
note: matrix. This case pins the VALUE half so neither notion can
note: drift into the other silently.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[probe
  dec-str=[$eq [u a=1.5] [u a='1.5']]
  atom-str=[$eq [u a=:ops] [u a='ops']]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[probe dec-str=true atom-str=true]
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-for-005-sort-limit level=core
  [tags for order-by limit]
  [in-cx [#
[users
  [user [name Carol]]
  [user [name Alice]]
  [user [name Bob]]
  [user [name Dave]]]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?for [user $u]
      [order-by $u/name]
      [limit 2]
      [yield $u]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[user [name 'Alice']]
[user [name 'Bob']]
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-order-by-desc-001 level=core
  [tags for order-by desc]
  [meta [#
note: probe #35 — descending sort direction. Direction accepts
note: BOTH the bare ident (`desc` / `asc`) immediately after the key expr
note: AND the colon-clause alias (`:desc` / `:asc`) — see
note: program-order-by-desc-002-colon-alias. Ties keep source order
note: (stable sort): C and B both age=30, C precedes B as in source.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $users [u-set [u name="C" age=30] [u name="A" age=25] [u name="B" age=30]]] [?for [in $u $users//u] [order-by $u/@age desc] [yield $u/@name]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'C'
'B'
'A'
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-order-by-desc-002-colon-alias level=core
  [tags for order-by desc colon-alias]
  [meta [#
note: The `:desc` colon-clause alias parses identically to the bare
note: `desc` form (every other for-comp modifier is a `:label`, so the
note: colon form reads naturally and is now accepted).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $users [u-set [u name="C" age=30] [u name="A" age=25] [u name="B" age=30]]] [?for [in $u $users//u] [order-by $u/@age desc] [yield $u/@name]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'C'
'B'
'A'
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-order-by-multikey-001 level=core
  [tags for order-by desc multi-key chained]
  [meta [#
note: probe #35 — `:then-by` does NOT exist, but a multi-key
note: sort is expressible by chaining `:order-by` clauses. Sort by the
note: SECONDARY key first, then the PRIMARY; the stable sort preserves
note: the secondary order within primary ties. Here: name asc, then age
note: desc → ties on age=30 stay name-ordered (B before C).
note: RE-PINNED (stream-2 W1, frame-set barrier pipeline): the prior
note: pin (A,B,C — name asc only) captured a DEFECT — the old engine
note: recursed per-frame after the first barrier, so the second
note: order-by saw one frame at a time and never sorted; chained
note: order-by clauses now actually chain, matching this note's own
note: stated semantics (primary = age desc → 30s first, tie stable).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $users [u-set [u name="C" age=30] [u name="A" age=25] [u name="B" age=30]]] [?for [in $u $users//u] [order-by $u/@name] [order-by $u/@age desc]
        [yield [r n=$u/@name a=$u/@age]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[r n=B a=30]
[r n=C a=30]
[r n=A a=25]
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-string-index-001 level=core
  [tags string substring char-access]
  [meta [#
note: probe #42 — char-level string access is via the
note: `substring(s, start, len)` builtin (1-indexed, XPath fn:substring
note: convention), NOT `$s[i]` subscript. Strings are scalars, not
note: sequences: `$s[1]` returns the whole string and `$s[2:4]` is empty
note: because `iterate(scalar)` yields a 1-element sequence. `substring`
note: is the blessed path for both single-char (len=1) and slice access.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $s "hello"] [$substring $s 2 3]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'ell'
#]]
 ]

