 [case id=program-for-013b-escaping-closure-zero-arg level=core
  [tags fn closure function-value escape]
  [doc [#
A closure RETURNED from a zero-argument [?def], bound, then applied resolves
its captured environment — the escaping anon travels WITH its value (§12.2.2;
cx-private #45 Bug-1). Before the embedded-closure-value fix the returned anon
was registered only in the (transient) call frame, so [$f 10] failed with
"no callable f". The zero-arg def is invoked with the call sigil [$mk] (a bare
0-arg head `[mk]` is a data element, not a call).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def mk () [?fn ($p) [+ $p 1]]]
[?let [= $f [$mk]] [$f 10]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
11
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-for-013c-nested-closure-recapture level=core
  [tags fn closure function-value escape composition]
  [doc [#
A closure produced by a combinator and then RE-CAPTURED into another closure
keeps its own captured environment (§12.2.2; cx-private #45 Bug-3). `pipe`
returns a Runnable; nesting `pipe` of a `pipe` is a >2-stage pipeline. Before
the fix the inner anon's environment was lost on re-capture (the program
crashed); the embedded-closure-value model resolves each anon against its own
defining scope. p2(10) = twice(inc(twice(10))) = twice(inc(20)) = twice(21) = 42.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def twice ($x) [+ $x $x]]
[?def inc ($x) [+ $x 1]]
[?def pipe ($f $g) [?fn ($x) [$g [$f $x]]]]
[?let [= $p2 [$pipe [$pipe $twice $inc] $twice]] [$p2 10]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
42
#]]
 ]

