 [case id=program-map-030-par-source-order-skewed level=core
  [tags map par source-order determinism L105 stream-5]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?map (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8) [using [?fn $x [?let [= $burn [$sum [$range 1 [* [- 9 $x] 20000]]]] [* $x 10]]]] [par]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
(10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80)
#]]
 ]
note: L105 discriminator — per-item work is skewed so earlier items
note: finish LAST under the pool; completion-order assembly would return
note: a permutation, source-order reassembly returns exactly this.

 [case id=program-for-030-par-source-order-skewed level=core
  [tags for par source-order determinism L105 stream-5]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[r [?for [in $i (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8)] [= $burn [$sum [$range 1 [* [- 9 $i] 20000]]]] [par] [yield [* $i 10]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[r 10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80]
#]]
 ]
note: the [?for … [par]] path (par_for_run) is distinct from the
note: [?map] par_map path — both must reassemble source order (L105).

 [case id=program-for-031-par-ordered-tombstone-noop level=core
  [tags for par ordered tombstone L105 stream-5]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[$eq
 [?for [in $i (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)] [par] [yield [* $i 7]]]
 [?for [in $i (1, 2, 3, 4, 5)] [par] [ordered] [yield [* $i 7]]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
true
#]]
 ]
note: [ordered] is TOMBSTONED (L105): a documented no-op — [par] always
note: preserves source order, so the two spellings are indistinguishable.
note: It remains grammatically valid where it was valid; [ordered]
note: without [par] stays CXER0100 (program-map-004).

 [case id=program-for-032-par-float-sum-source-order level=core
  [tags for par float determinism source-order L105 stream-5]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[$sum [?for [in $x (1.0e-1, 2.0e-1, 3.0e-1, 1.0e10, -1.0e10, 1.0e-1)] [par] [yield $x]]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
7.000003814697265e-1
#]]
 ]
note: float aggregation over a [par] result is SOURCE order — the sum is
note: byte-identical to the sequential fold (no reassociation, L105);
note: completion-order assembly would make this value nondeterministic.
note: The inputs MUST be spelled as floats (exponent form). They were bare
note: fixed-point, which autotypes DECIMAL (the 2b ruling) — and once #1017
note: made `[$sum]` fold the exact family exactly, this case answered 0.7
note: and stopped testing anything: exact decimal addition IS associative,
note: so no decimal sum can ever witness source order. The float artifact
note: in the pinned value is the whole witness; only a float carries it.

