 [case id=module-scope-frame-hof-sibling-dollar level=core
  [tags module scope fn closure let hof defining-scope issue-646]
  [doc [#
A [?fn] created under a [?let] frame inside a MODULE def and applied by a
HOF must resolve module siblings via the dollar form (#646). Derived frames
([?let]/[?loop]/[?for]/backtrack) dropped in_function_body +
cur_defining_scope, so the lambda captured the IMPORTING program's scope and
`[$bump]` failed with `no callable "bump"` — while the identical text ran
green as a program (the program scope happened to hold the defs there).
fold-bump(1,2,3) = (1+7)+(2+7)+(3+7) = 27.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib './scope-frames-module.cx' as=m]
[$m:fold-bump 1 2 3]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[total n=27]
#]]
 ]

 [case id=module-scope-frame-str-hole-deferred level=core
  [tags module scope fn closure let str interpolation defining-scope issue-646]
  [doc [#
[?str] interpolation inside a deferred [?fn] under a [?let] in a module def:
the sibling call [$offset] inside the lambda must resolve and the holes must
render (#646 class 1 — the CXER0100 non-scalar-hole raise was the sibling
call's err element flowing into the hole).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib './scope-frames-module.cx' as=m]
[$m:label-of 10]
#]]
  [out-text [#
'v=10|o=7'
#]]
 ]

 [case id=module-scope-frame-bare-sibling-call level=core
  [tags module scope fn closure let bare-call defining-scope issue-646]
  [doc [#
A BARE-form sibling call [bump $p] inside a deferred [?fn] in a module def is
a CALL, not data (#646 class 2 dual): with the defining scope dropped it
silently self-evaluated to a [bump 10] data element instead of invoking the
sibling — a silent wrong answer, worse than the dollar form's loud
`no callable`. bare-call(10) = 10+7 = 17.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib './scope-frames-module.cx' as=m]
[$m:bare-call 10]
#]]
  [out-text [#
17
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-def-nested-in-let-CXER0204 level=core
  [tags def nested let CXER0204 function-body issue-646]
  [doc [#
The §12.2 nested-def guard reaches THROUGH derived frames: a [?def] under a
[?let] inside a function body is still inside the function body (#646 dual —
the same dropped in_function_body flag that broke module scoping also let
this bypass CXER0204).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?def outer ()
  [?let [= $x 1]
    [?def inner () 2]]]
[$outer]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0204
#]]
 ]

