#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-cast-int-to-float level=core
  [tags cast int-to-float]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[cast 5 :float]
#]]
  [out-text [#
5.0e0
#]]
 ]

 [; #1010: this pair was ONE row named `program-cast-float-to-int-truncate`
    whose body was `[cast 3.7 :int]` — a DECIMAL literal, which does not
    truncate at all: it raises CXER0290. So the id was wrong about the kind AND
    the behavior, and — because the two kinds genuinely diverge here — the
    truncation the id named had no fixture anywhere. Both readings are now
    pinned, each under the kind it belongs to. ]
 [case id=program-cast-decimal-to-int-non-integral level=core
  [tags cast decimal-to-int non-integral CXER0290]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[cast 3.7 :int]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=cx-err:CXER0290 message='cast: decimal `3.7` is not integral — :int requires a whole value (L44)']
#]]
 ]

 [; #1016 — the POSITIVE half of L44's "decimal→int errors unless integral"
    had no fixture, so only the refusal was pinned and the clause read as if
    decimal never reached :int at all. Pinning both halves is what shows the
    rule is integrality, not kind. ]
 [case id=program-cast-decimal-to-int-integral level=core
  [tags cast decimal-to-int integral L44]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[cast 3.0 :int]
#]]
  [out-text [#
3
#]]
 ]

 [; #1016 — the `[cast X :int]` asymmetry the issue names is DELIBERATE and
    ruled on both sides, so both sides are pinned adjacently here. Float is
    approximate by construction, so narrowing truncates (code.md §6.5 P6
    coercion table, "Truncate toward zero"); decimal exists to be exact, so
    narrowing refuses unless it loses nothing (L44). Same spelling, different
    kind, different rule — by design. ]
 [case id=program-cast-float-to-int-truncate level=core
  [tags cast float-to-int truncate]
  [in-cx [#
[doc]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[cast 3.7e0 :int]
#]]
  [out-text [#
3
#]]
 ]

