 [; Numeric EBV (#382): cxdm.md §6 rule 2 — int and float share the numeric
    truthiness rule (value ≠ 0, and not NaN). Pinned across every boolean
    position: [?if], [?match] [where] guards, bare and $-head logical
    operators, and CXPath predicate bodies (which also pin the #384
    absence-marker rule: an absent attribute is the empty sequence, falsy).
    Each position is pinned TWICE (#1010) — once with a decimal literal
    (`…-decimal-0NN-…`) and once with an e-exponent float literal
    (`…-float-1NN-…`). The family carried only the first kind while every id
    claimed the second, so the float rule this note cites was, until #1010,
    asserted by a name and by nothing else.
    No NaN cases exist because CX floats are finite-only (normative,
    code.md §6.5): pure CX arithmetic raises CXER0101 before a NaN or
    ±Inf can arise, so the "(and not NaN)" EBV clause is FFI-defensive
    and unpinnable from pure CX. ]

 [case id=program-if-bare-then-else-err level=core
  [tags control-flow if malformed-body fail-closed CXER0100 error]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?if true 'a' 'b']
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0001
#]]
 ]

