 [case id=program-num-percent-001-modulo-head level=core
  [tags numeric mod operator percent]
  [; #598 (owner ruling a) — `%` is a reserved operator head aliasing the
     §6.5 mod builtin exactly: sign of dividend, $mod parity. ]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?str '{[% 7 3]}|{[% -7 3]}|{[= [% 10 4] [$mod 10 4]]}']
#]]
  [out-text [#
'1|-1|true'
#]]
 ]

 [; #1010 exposed this row's misnaming; #1016 fixed the behavior underneath it.
    History: it was ONE row named `program-num-percent-002-float-promotes`,
    annotated with #598's promotion ruling, whose body `[% 7.5 2]` is a DECIMAL.
    #1010 split it — 002 kept the body and pinned the OBSERVED refusal, 002a
    pinned the promotion — which then made the asymmetry #1016 names red-provable:
    `+ − × ÷` route decimals through the L44 exact lane while `%` alone still
    refused them. L44 rules `decimal ⊕ int` EXACT (only `decimal ⊕ float` is an
    error), so the refusal was a defect, not a rule, and 002 now pins the exact
    remainder. The refusal L44 DOES rule — the unbridged float mix — is 002b. ]
 [case id=program-num-percent-002-decimal-exact level=core
  [tags numeric mod operator percent decimal exact-family L44]
  [; #1016 / L44 — a decimal operand is EXACT, not refused and not promoted:
     `%` joins `+ − × ÷` in the exact lane. Scale is max(s₁,s₂), which L44
     forces by composition (the remainder is a subtraction, and the integral
     quotient contributes s₂), so `7.5 % 2` is `1.5` at scale 1. ]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[% 7.5 2]
#]]
  [out-text [#
1.5
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-num-percent-002b-decimal-float-unbridged level=core
  [tags numeric mod operator percent decimal float CXER0100 L44]
  [; #1016 / L44 — `decimal ⊕ float` is the refusal the spec DOES rule:
     silent coercion in either direction destroys the exactness the decimal
     kind exists for, so `[cast]` is the only bridge. Same message and code as
     the `+ − × ÷` lane, so the whole arithmetic surface refuses alike. ]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[% 7.5 2.0e0]
#]]
  [out-text [#
[err code=cx-err:CXER0100 message='%: decimal/bigint arithmetic admits only int/bigint/decimal operands — [cast] is the only decimal↔float bridge (L44)']
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-num-percent-002c-decimal-both-operands level=core
  [tags numeric mod operator percent decimal exact-family]
  [; #1016 — decimal ⊕ decimal. The remainder is zero but the SCALE survives
     (`0.0`, not `0`): decimal identity is scale-preserving, and the exact
     lane's `[- 7.5 7.5]` already renders `0.0` on the same max(s₁,s₂) rule. ]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[% 7.5 2.5]
#]]
  [out-text [#
0.0
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-num-percent-002d-bigint-exact level=core
  [tags numeric mod operator percent bigint exact-family]
  [; #1016 — the bigint half of the same lane. Pre-fix this refused with
     `mod: argument 1 is not numeric`; #38 had made bigint reject so it could
     not be silently i64-wrapped, and the exact lane is what makes the operand
     admissible without that loss. No decimal operand, so the result is bigint. ]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[% 9223372036854775808 3]
#]]
  [out-text [#
2
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-num-percent-002e-decimal-divide-by-zero level=core
  [tags numeric mod operator percent decimal error CXER0101]
