 [; #1044 / CO-17 — this row MOVED from the error-shape family to the exact
    lane, and its id changed with it, exactly as its `mod` twin below did in
    #1016. It pinned #38's refusal, which existed only because `idiv` had no
    exact path and an over-i64 dividend would otherwise have been silently
    i64-wrapped. #1016 left it refusing with a NAMED reason — "giving it one
    needs its own ruling on result kind" — and CO-17 is that ruling: `idiv`
    yields an integer per §6.5, in the exact family's integral representation
    (int while it fits i64, bigint past it, never a saturated i64).
    Read this row WITH its `mod` twin: 7 × 1428571428571428571 + 3 is exactly
    10000000000000000000, so the two rows together pin the divmod identity
    over an operand no i64 or f64 lane can hold. ]
 [case id=program-num-idiv-bigint-exact level=core
  [tags numeric idiv bigint exact-family L44 CO-17]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[$idiv 10000000000000000000 7]
#]]
  [out-text [#
1428571428571428571
#]]
 ]

 [; #1016 — this row MOVED from the error-shape family to the exact lane, and
    its id changed with it: `mod` over a bigint is no longer an error at all.
    It pinned #38's refusal, which existed only because `mod` had no exact path
    and an over-i64 dividend would otherwise have been silently i64-wrapped.
    L44 rules the operand pair exact, so the exact lane — not refusal — is what
    keeps #38's value intact. ]
 [case id=program-num-mod-bigint-exact level=core
  [tags numeric mod bigint exact-family L44]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[$mod 10000000000000000000 7]
#]]
  [out-text [#
3
#]]
 ]

