[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $bad 0x2a::decimal] $bad]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
 ]

 [doc [#
## Fixtures (cxpath kind tests — grammar [131b], `spec/core/ast.md`
## node-test table)

The four kind tests test node KINDS, not names:

| Test          | Matches |
|---|---|
| `node()`      | any node |
| `text()`      | Text nodes only |
| `element()`   | element nodes only |
| `attribute()` | attribute nodes only |

`attribute()` is therefore non-empty on the ATTRIBUTE AXIS alone — no
other axis carries attribute nodes (XPath 3.1 §3.3.2). Every one of the
four was unreachable before issue-809: in expression position
`$t/node()` misparsed as a path plus an empty sequence literal, and in
binding position it was a hard parse error.
#]]

 [case id=program-conc-005a-try-send-malformed-timeout-refused level=concurrency
  [tags channel try-send timeout negative CXER0100 issue-816]
  [meta [#
note: issue-816 — code.md §10.4.2 defines `timeout=DURATION` on both
note: try-variants and the engine read only to= / from=, so the deadline
note: was accepted and dropped: a zero-wait poll wearing a timeout's
note: name. The #793 class through a different door.
note:
note: The deadline is now honoured, which makes a MALFORMED one a real
note: error rather than a second silent drop — answering a
note: silently-ignored argument with another silent ignore would be no
note: fix at all. The duration is read by the same reader [?timeout] and
note: [?circuit-breaker] use, so one spelling of a duration cannot mean
note: two things.
note:
note: The WAIT itself is asserted in vcx/tests (a fixture cannot read a
note: clock); these pin the surface contract.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $ch [?channel name="c5a" buffer=1]] [?try-send "x" to=$ch timeout=soon]]
#]]
  [out-err [#
cx-err:CXER0100
#]]
 ]

