 [case id=program-conc-041-try-send-reaches-a-fan-out-subscriber level=concurrency
  [tags channel try-send fan-out regression silent-loss]
  [meta [#
note: REGRESSION — [?try-send] onto a fan-out channel (sharing=independent)
note: answered [ok] while the value sat in the GROUP queue, where no
note: subscriber ever looks: a SILENT LOSS. [?send] and the [?select] send
note: case both split on sharing; try-send did not. A fan-out send never
note: blocks and is never buffer-bound (the window is retention, not flow),
note: so there is no timeout path on this arm. Same class as the scheduler's
note: dead tick wire (SUP-1e, conformance/stdlib/sched.cxd sched-039).
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $ch [?channel name="ts-fan" sharing=independent retention=window keep=3]]
[= $sub [?subscribe from=$ch]]
[= $a [?send [ev 1] to=$ch]]
[= $b [?try-send [ev 2] to=$ch]]
[= $r1 [?receive from=$sub]]
[= $r2 [?receive from=$sub max=5 deadline=0]]
  ($a, $b, $r1, $r2)]
#]]
  [out-text [#
([ok], [ok], [ev 1], ([ev 2]))
#]]
 ]


 [case id=program-array-042-discrete-token-array level=core
  [tags array literal separator asp-2 issue-903 data-reading]
  [meta [#
note: RULED ASP-2 (#903) — a comma-less DATA bracket whose 2+ ws-delimited
note: tokens are all discrete values (typed scalars, quoted strings, holes,
note: `[…]` nodes, `(…)` sequences, map-shaped `{…}`) is an ARRAY of those
note: values. Before ASP-2 the `(`/`{` tokens fell to the comma path, whose
note: slot reader glued the run into ONE nested item and mangled the leading
note: value per type: the int 1 became the STRING '1 ' (trailing space and
note: all), a quoted string lost its quoting, `[1 (2)]` refused outright.
note: Every wrong shape was SILENT and round-tripped stably, so
note: canonicalization could not reveal it. The canonical rendering is the
note: sharpest pin: each shape must come back as separate comma-joined items
note: with its leading value's TYPE intact (`[1, (2, 3)]`, not
note: `[('1 ', (2, 3))]`). `g` pins the glued exception: a span NOT
note: ws-delimited on both sides is not a token, so `[(1, 2)[0]]` keeps its
note: historical one-item mixed reading (the same "glued runs are one item"
note: rule that keeps CXPath kind tests `$c/node()` parsing, fmt-013).
note: `h` pins the element guard: a name-shaped first token keeps the
note: element reading, so `[true (2, 3)]` is the ELEMENT `true`.
note:
note: The subject is the DATA reader. The PROGRAM reader is deliberately NOT
note: covered: there a bracket is an array only when it carries a top-level
note: comma, because it must also disambiguate patterns, calls and elements
note: — `[1 2 3]` in program position is not an array literal and still
note: refuses. A reader difference by design, recorded so it is not
note: mistaken for drift.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[doc [a [1 (2, 3)]] [b [1 (2)]] [c [(1) (2)]] [d [1 2 3]] [e [1 {a: 1}]] [f [{a: 1} {b: 2}]] [g [(1, 2)[0]]] [h [true (2, 3)]]]
#]]
  [in-code [#
$doc
#]]
  [out-text [#
[doc [a [1, (2, 3)]] [b [1, (2)]] [c [(1), (2)]] [d [1, 2, 3]] [e [1, {a: 1}]] [f [{a: 1}, {b: 2}]] [g [((1, 2), [0])]] [h [true (2, 3)]]]
#]]
 ]

 [case id=program-array-043-element-body-discrete-children level=core
  [tags element body typed-list separator asp-3 issue-909 data-reading]
  [meta [#
note: RULED ASP-3 (#909) — the ASP-2 discrete-token rule extends to ELEMENT
note: BODIES: a comma-less body whose 2+ ws-delimited tokens are all discrete
note: values (typed scalars, quoted strings, holes, `[…]` nodes, `(…)`
note: sequences, map-shaped `{…}`) yields those values as DISCRETE CHILDREN.
note: Before ASP-3 such a body fell to the prose lane, which restringified
note: the leading scalar: `[k 1 (2, 3)]` came back `[k '1' (2, 3)]` (the int
note: became the STRING '1 ', trailing space and all); bools and atoms
note: corrupted the same way. The mangled shape is ENGINE OUTPUT — stdlib
note: ux-016 renders `[list false (verb, label, …) …]`, so the engine's own
note: output failed its own re-parse (the #704 class), invisibly, because no
note: gate re-reads rendered output. `l` pins that ux-016 shape re-reading as
note: a bool child plus two sequences. `p` pins the bareword guard: any
note: bareword keeps the WHOLE body its historical prose/mixed reading,
note: byte-identically. `g` pins the glued exception: a span not ws-delimited
note: on both sides is not a token. Unlike the HEADLESS position (case 042
note: `h`), there is NO name-shaped first-token guard here — an element body
note: has no element reading to protect, and ux-016's own body starts with
note: the name-shaped bool `false`.
#]]
  [in-cx [#
[doc [k 1 (2, 3)] [l false (verb, label, target) (label, open, url)] [m {a: 1} {b: 2}] [p weight (kg, lbs) shown] [g (1, 2)[0]]]
#]]
  [in-code [#
$doc
#]]
  [out-text [#
[doc [k 1 (2, 3)] [l false (verb, label, target) (label, open, url)] [m {a: 1} {b: 2}] [p 'weight' (kg, lbs) ' shown'] [g (1, 2) [0]]]
#]]
 ]

 [; ============================================================================
    The path/value matrix grid (R-A1..R-A6, 2026-08-25) — one case per ruled
    cell of the FROZEN matrix in code.md §6.2 / ledger matrix file. Changing
    any expectation below requires a ruling naming the cell. ]

 [case id=program-pvmatrix-001-seq-wildcard-distributes level=core
  [tags cxpath pvmatrix distribution sequence wildcard]
  [; R-A6(i): /* on a collection is the union of the members' children,
     never the member list (was 2 — the members — before the ruling). ]
  [in-cx [#
[ignored]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?let [= $s ([a [n 1]], [b [n 2] [n 3]])] [$count $s/*]]
#]]
  [out-text [#
3
#]]
 ]

